            <ArticleTitle>Recurrent back pain and fevers.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>208-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>An elderly woman presented with her third episode of back pain, fever and neurological deficits. She subsequently developed pseudogout of the knee, and a diagnosis was made of systemic calcium pyrophosphate deposition disease (CPPD) involving the spine and causing intermittent cauda equina syndrome. This case illustrates the difficulty of differentiating infection from CPPD.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
